// Copper's admin group is wheel, not sudo or admin.
//
// The first-boot wizard puts the session user in wheel, and the shipped
// sudoers is the line that grants wheel root through sudo. polkit does not
// read sudoers: its stock rules (49-ubuntu-admin, 50-default from polkitd)
// name unix-group:sudo and unix-group:admin, neither of which exists here.
// Without this file polkit treats the only real account as a non-admin and
// every auth_admin action -- mount a removable drive, change the power
// profile, add a network connection -- prompts for the root password, which
// is locked on a live image. The prompt then cannot be satisfied at all.
//
// Rules are read in filename order and the first admin rule that returns a
// non-empty list wins, so the 10- prefix deliberately runs before Ubuntu's
// 49- and 50-. Referencing the group by name keeps it correct even if the
// build allocates wheel a different gid.
polkit.addAdminRule(function(action, subject) {
    return ["unix-group:wheel"];
});
